{
  "identifier": "NG806",
  "full_title": "Christ carrying the Cross and the Virgin Mary Swooning",
  "short_title": "Christ carrying the Cross and the Virgin Mary Swooning",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "high Renaissance",
  "material": "oil on wood",
  "dimensions": "136.6 × 134.6 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1501",
  "brief_description": "This is thought to be one of Boccaccino's earliest known works. It is an altarpiece painted for the choir screen of the church of S. Domenico in Cremona.\n\nChrist carries the Cross escorted by three soldiers, two of whom are pulling the rope around his neck. The procession winds up the hill, on top of which two crosses have been erected. The two thieves who will be crucified beside Jesus walk ahead wearing only loincloths. Saint John, dressed in red, clasps his hands and looks down at the Virgin who has fainted and is surrounded by the three Marys, who were later to discover Christ's empty tomb.\n\nSaint John's position in the centre of the altarpiece suggests he was the subject of special devotion by the person who commissioned it. The  swooning (lo spasimo in Italian) of the Virgin is an episode shown in a number of sixteenth-century Italian paintings of Christ carrying the Cross, and this altar in S. Domenico was dedicated to the mystery.",
  "accession_date": "1870-01-01",
